{
  "topic": "Mecánica Clásica",
  "harvestedAt": "2026-07-31T19:26:51.719Z",
  "entries": [
    {
      "id": "arx-1502.01991v1-k0",
      "type": "key_fact",
      "text": "We show that, despite focusing on such a narrow period of time, so close to those seminal years, practically all the questions which still prevail nowadays, were then already posed.",
      "source": "arxiv",
      "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.01991v1",
      "sourceTitle": "Atomismo y Mecánica Cuántica",
      "tier": "TIER_1_ACADEMIC",
      "authorityScore": 95,
      "relevanceScore": 75
    },
    {
      "id": "arx-2101.11944v1-k1",
      "type": "key_fact",
      "text": "The objective here is to help understand the core of the PSO paradigm from the bottom up by offering some insight into the form of the particles' trajectories, and to provide some guidelines as to how to decide upon the settings of the coefficients in the particles' velocity update equation in the proposed formulation to obtain the type of behaviour desired for the problem at hand.",
      "source": "arxiv",
      "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.11944v1",
      "sourceTitle": "Coefficients' Settings in Particle Swarm Optimization: Insight and Guidelines",
      "tier": "TIER_1_ACADEMIC",
      "authorityScore": 95,
      "relevanceScore": 75
    },
    {
      "id": "arx-1701.07104v1-k2",
      "type": "key_fact",
      "text": "In previous work, we derived the most general solution of the collisionless Boltzmann equation describing the accretion of a kinetic gas into a Schwarzschild black hole background, and we gave explicit expressions for the corresponding observables (the current density and stress energy-momentum tensor) in terms of certain integrals over the distribution function.",
      "source": "arxiv",
      "sourceUrl": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.07104v1",
      "sourceTitle": "Spherical steady-state accretion of a relativistic collisionless gas into a Schwarzschild black hole",
      "tier": "TIER_1_ACADEMIC",
      "authorityScore": 95,
      "relevanceScore": 75
    },
    {
      "id": "wp-es-549660-d0",
      "type": "definition",
      "text": "La mecánica clásica es la rama de la física que estudia las leyes del comportamiento de cuerpos físicos macroscópicos (a diferencia de la mecánica cuántica) en reposo y a velocidades pequeñas comparadas con la velocidad de la luz.",
      "source": "wikipedia",
      "sourceUrl": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mec%C3%A1nica_cl%C3%A1sica",
      "sourceTitle": "Mecánica clásica",
      "tier": "TIER_3_HIGH_QUALITY_WEB",
      "authorityScore": 75,
      "relevanceScore": 90
    },
    {
      "id": "wp-es-549660-d1",
      "type": "definition",
      "text": "En la mecánica clásica en general existen tres aspectos invariantes: el tiempo es absoluto, la evolución temporal de los sistemas se realiza de acuerdo  con el principio de mínima acción y las leyes físicas son deterministas.",
      "source": "wikipedia",
      "sourceUrl": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mec%C3%A1nica_cl%C3%A1sica",
      "sourceTitle": "Mecánica clásica",
      "tier": "TIER_3_HIGH_QUALITY_WEB",
      "authorityScore": 75,
      "relevanceScore": 90
    },
    {
      "id": "wp-es-549660-d2",
      "type": "definition",
      "text": "Consiste en los conceptos físicos basados en los trabajos fundacionales de Sir Isaac Newton, y en los métodos matemáticos inventados por Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, Joseph-Louis Lagrange, Leonhard Euler, y otros contemporáneos, en el siglo XVII para describir el movimiento de los cuerpos físicos bajo la influencia de un sistema de fuerzas.",
      "source": "wikipedia",
      "sourceUrl": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mec%C3%A1nica_cl%C3%A1sica",
      "sourceTitle": "Mecánica clásica",
      "tier": "TIER_3_HIGH_QUALITY_WEB",
      "authorityScore": 75,
      "relevanceScore": 90
    },
